	\begin{abstract}
		Real-time systems often combine predictable periodic activities with irregular aperiodic requests. If aperiodic work is executed without control, it can disturb hard real-time deadlines; if it is executed only in background time, response time may become unacceptable. The Sporadic Server is a fixed-priority server mechanism designed to serve aperiodic requests while preserving the temporal protection of periodic hard real-time tasks. This paper explains the server-capacity and replenishment idea, presents a compact UPPAAL model of the main budget-consumption mechanism, compares the Sporadic Server with related fixed-priority servers, and evaluates its assumptions, overheads, and application limits. The conclusion is that the Sporadic Server is useful but assumption-dependent: it improves aperiodic responsiveness while bounding interference, but it requires correct replenishment handling, accurate timing information, bounded workload, and manageable scheduler overhead.
	\end{abstract}
	
	\begin{IEEEkeywords}
		Real-time systems, fixed-priority scheduling, aperiodic tasks, Sporadic Server, scheduling servers, UPPAAL.
	\end{IEEEkeywords}
	
	\section{Introduction}
	
	Hard real-time systems are designed under the condition that missing a deadline can violate system correctness. In real-time computing, correctness depends not only on the logical value of a result but also on the time at which the result is produced; therefore, predictability is more important than high average throughput \cite[Sec.~1.2]{buttazzo2011hard}. Classical scheduling theory provides the basis for analyzing such systems, especially for periodic hard real-time tasks \cite{liu1973scheduling}. However, realistic embedded systems also receive irregular requests, such as diagnostic messages, alarm events, communication packets, or operator commands.
	
	This creates a scheduling problem. Aperiodic requests should be served quickly, but they must not consume processor time in a way that invalidates periodic timing guarantees. Executing them only as background work is simple and safe but may lead to poor response time. Executing them at high priority without control improves responsiveness but may endanger periodic deadlines. The Sporadic Server addresses this problem by reserving bounded execution capacity for aperiodic work and replenishing consumed capacity in a controlled way \cite{sprunt1989aperiodic}.
	
	The purpose of this paper is to explain the Sporadic Server, position it among related fixed-priority servers, demonstrate its main mechanism in UPPAAL, and critically evaluate when it is useful in embedded real-time systems.
	
	\section{Background and Problem Setting}
	
	A periodic task releases jobs at regular intervals and is usually described by a period, deadline, and worst-case execution time. This model fits many control and monitoring functions. Aperiodic tasks, in contrast, arrive irregularly; their exact arrival times are not known in advance. These task models form the basis for server-based scheduling in real-time systems \cite[Sec.~2.2]{buttazzo2011hard}. The combination of hard periodic tasks with soft or irregular aperiodic requests is the main motivation for fixed-priority server mechanisms \cite{sprunt1989aperiodic}.
	
	Background scheduling is the simplest solution: aperiodic work executes only when no periodic task is ready. This protects periodic tasks, but aperiodic response time can become large. A server improves this by reserving a controlled amount of processor capacity for aperiodic work. The challenge is to manage this capacity so that aperiodic response improves without destroying the schedulability assumptions of the periodic task set.
	
	The Sporadic Server is normally discussed in a fixed-priority preemptive context. The server itself receives a priority and is analyzed as part of the workload. This is important because the server must not behave like an uncontrolled high-priority task; its budget and replenishment rule must bound the processor demand it can create \cite{sprunt1989aperiodic}.
	
	\section{Core Idea of the Sporadic Server}
	
	The Sporadic Server is characterized by a server capacity $C_s$ and a server period $T_s$. The capacity is the maximum processor time the server may use for aperiodic jobs before its budget is exhausted. The period governs the replenishment rule that restores consumed capacity after a delay. Aperiodic jobs may execute at the server priority only while capacity remains \cite{sprunt1989aperiodic}.
	
	The distinctive feature is replenishment. Unlike the Polling and Deferrable Servers, which restore capacity to full value at period boundaries, the Sporadic Server replenishes only the capacity that has actually been consumed \cite[Sec.~5.6]{buttazzo2011hard}. When the server becomes active while capacity remains, the next replenishment time is associated with that activation. The amount restored later equals the capacity consumed during the corresponding interval. This delayed, consumption-based replenishment bounds the processor demand generated by aperiodic work.
	
	Because consumed capacity is restored only after a delay, the server can be accounted for in fixed-priority schedulability analysis like a controlled workload. Under the model assumptions, the periodic utilization bound is preserved, and an upper bound on the admissible server size can be derived \cite[Sec.~5.6, Eq.~(5.24)]{buttazzo2011hard}. Thus, the server is responsive when budget exists but prevents aperiodic work from continuously taking processor time.
	
	\section{UPPAAL Model of the Main Mechanism}
	\label{sec:uppaal_model}
	
	To demonstrate the main feature of the Sporadic Server, a simplified UPPAAL model was developed. The model is not a complete implementation of the original algorithm; it is a formal demonstration of bounded server budget, delayed replenishment, aperiodic request service, and protection of a higher-priority periodic task.
	
	The model contains two templates. A \textit{Clock} template generates a discrete \texttt{tick} every time unit, similar to a timer interrupt in a real-time kernel. A \textit{Scheduler} template receives this tick and performs one atomic update: replenishment handling, periodic release, aperiodic arrival, scheduling decision, budget consumption, and deadline checking. The fixed-priority order is
	\[
	\text{Periodic task} > \text{Sporadic Server}.
	\]
	Therefore, the periodic task executes before the server whenever it is active. The server executes aperiodic work only if the aperiodic queue is non-empty and the server budget is positive.
	
