	\section{Related Approaches and Tradeoffs}
	
	Buttazzo treats background scheduling, the Polling Server, the Deferrable Server, Priority Exchange, the Sporadic Server, and Slack Stealing as fixed-priority server approaches for hybrid workloads consisting of hard periodic and soft or firm aperiodic activity \cite[Ch.~5]{buttazzo2011hard}. They address the same basic problem but differ in how reserved capacity is preserved, consumed, and replenished.
	
	Background scheduling is very simple and strongly protects periodic jobs because it only uses idle time. Its weakness is poor aperiodic response time, especially when periodic utilization is high \cite[Sec.~5.2]{buttazzo2011hard}. The Polling Server periodically checks for pending aperiodic work and serves it using its capacity, but capacity can be lost if no request is pending at the polling instant \cite[Sec.~5.3]{buttazzo2011hard}. The Deferrable Server preserves capacity within its period, improving responsiveness, but this creates a different interference pattern that must be analyzed carefully \cite{strosnider1995deferrable}, \cite[Sec.~5.4]{buttazzo2011hard}.
	
	Priority Exchange preserves otherwise-unused server capacity by trading it down to lower-priority periodic execution, requiring more involved bookkeeping across priority levels \cite[Sec.~5.5]{buttazzo2011hard}. Slack Stealing is more aggressive because it uses available slack in the periodic schedule, often giving strong average response but requiring computation of slack \cite[Sec.~5.7]{buttazzo2011hard}.
	
	The Sporadic Server is therefore best understood as a balance between responsiveness and analyzability, not as the universally fastest method. Buttazzo's performance discussion indicates that Deferrable Server, Priority Exchange, and Sporadic Server all reduce average aperiodic response compared with background and polling service, while the Sporadic Server may be slightly slower on average than Deferrable Server and Priority Exchange because consumed capacity is restored only one server period $T_s$ after consumption \cite[Sec.~5.9]{buttazzo2011hard}.
	
