	\section{Critical Evaluation}
	
	The main strength of the Sporadic Server is that it provides bounded processor capacity for aperiodic work, improving response time over background execution while protecting periodic hard real-time tasks \cite{sprunt1989aperiodic}. The parameters $C_s$ and $T_s$ give the designer a concrete way to reason about how much processor time is reserved for irregular requests. A second strength is analyzability: since the server has defined capacity and replenishment behaviour, it can be included in fixed-priority timing analysis under the assumptions of the model \cite{liu1973scheduling}.
	
	The most important limitation is implementation complexity. The scheduler must track remaining capacity, reduce it during aperiodic execution, create replenishment events, restore capacity at the correct time, and manage pending requests. Timing inaccuracy is another risk. Timer resolution, interrupt latency, context-switch cost, and operating-system overhead can disturb the ideal replenishment model. Restoring capacity too early or measuring consumed capacity too coarsely can make the real server interfere more than expected.
	
	The Sporadic Server also does not solve overload. It bounds how much service aperiodic work receives, but if aperiodic demand continuously exceeds the reserved capacity, the queue grows and response time degrades. Buttazzo treats overload as a separate problem requiring explicit handling mechanisms \cite[Ch.~9]{buttazzo2011hard}. In addition, shared resources must be controlled. If periodic and aperiodic tasks share locks, buffers, or devices, blocking and priority inversion may affect timing; protocols such as priority inheritance and priority ceiling are therefore relevant \cite{sha1990priorityinheritance}.
	
	Buttazzo emphasizes that fixed-priority servers should be compared not only by response-time performance but also by computational complexity, memory requirement, and implementation complexity \cite[Sec.~5.10]{buttazzo2011hard}. The UPPAAL model in Section~\ref{sec:uppaal_model} illustrates these overhead sources abstractly: even the simplified model needs variables for current budget, queue length, consumed capacity, replenishment countdown, and served jobs. Practical implementation additionally depends on kernel overhead, which must be considered in guarantee tests \cite[Sec.~10.7]{buttazzo2011hard}.
	
	\section{Embedded-System Application Discussion}
	
	A suitable application is an automotive electronic control unit with periodic control tasks and irregular diagnostic communication. The periodic functions require predictable timing, while diagnostic or status messages arrive irregularly. The Sporadic Server is useful if these messages require better-than-background response but must remain bounded so control tasks stay protected. A second example is an industrial controller whose periodic loops sample sensors and update actuators while alarms or operator commands occur sporadically. The UPPAAL model represents this pattern abstractly: one hard periodic task models the protected control activity, while aperiodic arrivals represent irregular diagnostic or alarm requests served through bounded server capacity.
	
	The approach is less suitable under sustained aperiodic overload, highly dynamic workloads, or very small microcontrollers with simple schedulers and limited timer support. In such systems, the cost of budget accounting and replenishment management may outweigh the benefit. The method is strongest when the periodic core is predictable and the irregular workload is bounded.
	
	\section{Open Issues and Possible Extensions}
	
	One open issue is the gap between the theoretical server model and a real RTOS implementation. A practical study could measure the effect of timer granularity, interrupt latency, and context-switch overhead on server behaviour. Another direction is overload-aware service: the server controls how much processor time aperiodic work receives, but it does not decide which jobs should be dropped, delayed, or degraded during overload. A natural extension is the dynamic-priority counterpart under EDF, where the replenishment idea appears as the Dynamic Sporadic Server \cite[Sec.~6.3]{buttazzo2011hard}. Finally, real systems should evaluate server behaviour together with resource-access protocols, otherwise blocking can invalidate timing assumptions.
	
