	\section{Conclusion}
	
	The Sporadic Server is a useful mechanism for fixed-priority hard real-time systems that must serve aperiodic requests without sacrificing the timing protection of periodic tasks. Its strength is the combination of reserved service capacity and delayed, consumption-based replenishment, which improves aperiodic response over background execution while keeping interference analyzable. It is not universally best: on average response time it can fall slightly behind the Deferrable Server and Priority Exchange, while Slack Stealing can outperform all of them at high aperiodic load.
	
	The approach is well suited to embedded systems with a predictable periodic core and bounded irregular events, such as controllers with occasional diagnostics or alarms. It is less suited to continuous aperiodic overload, highly dynamic systems, or platforms without accurate timing support. Its practical value depends on known task parameters, bounded execution times, accurate budget accounting, correct replenishment handling, and manageable scheduler overhead.
	
